- Initialize an empty array (`digits`).
- Start processing numbers up to one million.
- Keep dividing the remaining part of `currentNumber` by ten until only whole numbers remain (i.e., `[9999]`, after which further division won’t change anything since `.toString()` removes leading zeros).

Each iteration adds the last unpaired digit to the result array (`digits`). Finally, reversing the resulting array gives you exactly what was requested—each digit sequentially without any intermediate padding issues due to floating-point arithmetic precision limitations inherent when converting integers directly to strings using methods like `.toString()`.
